# elohim-render/src/shim — the V8 runtime shims

Every `.js` file here is compiled INTO the binary by deno_core's `js = [dir "...", "..."]`
extension macro and installed onto one long-lived isolate's `globalThis`. The module-level
orientation (what each shim covers, install ordering, the deliberate spec divergences) lives
co-located in `mod.rs`; this manifest records the two governance facts an editor needs and
why neither becomes an edit-time gate.

## Considered, no enforcing rule (the deliberate opt-in, not a nag)

**1. ASCII purity is an INVARIANT — and it is already caught by the strongest possible gate.**
Every `.js` file here must be pure ASCII. The macro uses `ascii_str_include!`, which panics at
**const-eval time** on any non-ASCII byte with a cryptic
`error[E0080]: evaluation panicked: assertion failed: buffer.is_ascii() && ...` naming no file
and no line. The offenders are always prose habits — em-dash, en-dash, curly quotes, ellipsis,
arrows, non-breaking space — in *comments*. The fix is the HTML entity escape (`&#x2014;` for
em-dash, `&#x2018;`/`&#x2019;` for curly quotes), documented at the top of `mod.rs`.

This is not gated here because the compile itself is an exhaustive, unbypassable structural
backstop — strictly stronger than any edit-time advisory, which could only enumerate the
*common* offending characters via `contains-any` and would miss the rest. What the compiler
gives poorly is the *message*, not the *catch*; a `contains-any` rule would need a registered
validator-EPR to be actionable at all (schema: an enforcing rule requires one of
`require-frontmatter`/`route-to`/`validator`/…), and an unregistered one degrades to an inert
advisory — the footgun `validate_meta` exists to prevent. Sibling precedent for this outcome
on a code tree: `doorway/doorway-service/.epr-meta`.

Scar tissue: this trap fired on 2026-07-30 from an in-flight `url.js` edit (three em-dashes in
new comments), turning the whole crate red mid-verification for an unrelated session.

**2. Request-scoped state must not be cached on `globalThis`.** These shims live on an isolate
that is **reused across sequential renders** — only the `DataFetcher` is swapped between them
(`runtime.rs::set_fetcher`), and nothing resets the JS heap. `web_api.js`'s install guard
(`if (typeof globalThis[name] === "undefined")`) correctly prevents re-installation from
clobbering live state, and is exactly why nothing is reset between renders: shims install once
per isolate, not once per render.

So a shim that memoizes per-request data on the global object adds a lane to a live
cross-principal residue channel — doorway already renders authenticated requests with a
per-user credentialed fetcher (`FetcherTrust::Principal`). Keep request-scoped state in
`OpState`, which IS swapped per render. This is a design judgment about what a new shim
*means*, not a mechanizable predicate over file content — there is no textual signature that
distinguishes legitimate one-time install state from illegitimate per-request caching — so it
is held by the `mod.rs` doc contract and review, and by the trust bookkeeping and tests in
`elohim/elohim-render/tests/isolate_trust.rs`.

Full context and the deno_core-realm verdict:
`genesis/data/timeline/backlog/elohim-render-isolate-reuse-trust-boundary.md`.
